---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/immigration-detention/">Immigration detention</a><span>October 1, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Immigration detention &middot; October 1, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#his-case">One man's case</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-changed">What changed</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-fight">The fight over two phrases</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Supreme Court agreed on October 1 to decide whether federal law requires the government to keep certain immigrants in detention for the whole of their deportation cases.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /> The case concerns people who crossed into the country without being inspected and admitted, and who were arrested years later inside the country.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" /> The Court has not ruled, and it has not scheduled arguments.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">For about thirty years, a person in that situation could ask an immigration judge to be released on bond while the case went on.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="For nearly thirty years, that request would have been granted" /> The Trump administration ended that in July 2025, saying the 1996 immigration law requires holding them until their cases end.<Cite s="ice-interim-guidance-2025-07-08" passage="has revisited its legal position on detention and release authorities" /> The only release left is parole, which the Department of Homeland Security grants case by case, for "urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit."<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Tens of thousands of detained people have sued in federal court since then.<Cite s="politico-detention-tracker-2026-10-06" passage="At least 20,250 of those were rulings against the administration" /> The federal appeals courts, called circuits, have split. Of the eleven that have ruled, nine say the administration's reading is wrong, so federal judges in most of the country order bond hearings for the people who sue.<Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies" /> The other two, the Fifth and Eighth Circuits, agreed with the administration, and detained people in Texas and Louisiana have a harder time getting released.<Cite s="ap-fourth-circuit-2026-09-10" passage="including Texas and Louisiana, face additional hurdles to seek release" /> The Second Circuit wrote that the government's reading would mean detention without bond "for millions of noncitizens."<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="for millions of noncitizens" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="his-case">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">One man's case</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha came to the United States from Brazil in 2004 or 2005, without being inspected at the border.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="entered the United States without inspection and admission over twenty years ago (in either 2004 or 2005)" /> He has lived in Massachusetts since then with his wife and their two children, who are U.S. citizens. He owns his home and runs a small construction business.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="He lives in Massachusetts with his wife and two U.S.-citizen children, owns his home, and owns and runs a small construction business." /> He applied for asylum in 2016. The application is still pending, and it gave him permission to work.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="In 2016, he applied for asylum and was granted work authorization while his application was under review." /> He has never been arrested or charged with a crime.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner has never been arrested for or charged with a crime." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 26, 2025, immigration officers arrested him as he drove to work in Norwood, Massachusetts, and took him to an Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) detention center in Buffalo, New York.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="moved to an immigration detention facility in Buffalo, New York" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">He asked for a bond hearing, and an immigration judge refused.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied" /> Immigration judges work for the Justice Department and must follow the department's Board of Immigration Appeals, and three weeks earlier the board had ruled that they cannot hold these hearings.<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests" /> So he sued in federal court. After a hearing on October 20, a federal judge in Rochester, New York, ordered the government to give him a bond hearing within ten days.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="The Court held a virtual hearing" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="within ten days of its initial text order" /> On October 28, 2025, an immigration judge held it, found he was neither a danger nor a flight risk, and released him on bond. He went home the next day, after {computed.daysDetained} days in detention.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="A bond hearing was held on October 28, 2025, and respondent was released on bond the next day." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government did not argue he was dangerous. It argued the law required holding him anyway, and it appealed.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="whom the government concedes poses no danger to the community nor risk of flight" /> On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit, the federal appeals court for New York, ruled for him and held that his detention falls under the section of the law that allows bond.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="detention is governed by Section 1226(a)" /> His deportation case was still open, and his next date in immigration court was June 28, 2027. Under the government's reading he would have been held until at least then.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="next hearing is scheduled for June 28, 2027" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government asked the Supreme Court to take the case in July.<Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Petition for a writ of certiorari filed" /> In September the Second Circuit declined to have its full bench rehear it.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." /> Judge Steven Menashi dissented.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="I dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>He first sued in federal court in Massachusetts. Because ICE had moved him to Buffalo, the case was transferred to the federal court in western New York, which sits under the Second Circuit.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="he had already been transported to an ICE detention facility in Buffalo" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="aclu-release-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>His lawyer, Paul Dwyer, said Barbosa da Cunha "has never missed an immigration appointment or hearing."<Cite s="aclu-release-2026-10-01" passage="He has never missed an immigration appointment or hearing." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-changed">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What changed</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The 1996 law's detention rules took effect in 1997, and that year the government wrote a rule saying people who entered without inspection "will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination."<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination" /> Five presidents kept that practice.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years and across five Presidential administrations" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On January 20, 2025, President Trump ordered the Department of Homeland Security to hold people arrested for immigration violations until their cases are decided, "to the extent permitted by law," and to "allocate all legally available resources" to detention facilities.<Cite s="eo-14159-2025-01-20" passage="to the extent permitted by law" /><Cite s="eo-14159-2025-01-20" passage="allocate all legally available resources" /> On July 4 he signed a budget law with $45 billion for detention space.<Cite s="plaw-119-21-2025-07-04" passage="$45,000,000,000, for single adult alien detention capacity" /> Four days later, ICE told every employee that the department, "in coordination with the Department of Justice," had "revisited its legal position." From then on, people who entered without inspection could not have a bond hearing.<Cite s="ice-interim-guidance-2025-07-08" passage="has revisited its legal position on detention and release authorities" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The memo became public on July 15. That day the department posted on X:<Cite s="cbs-ice-bond-hearings-2025-07-15" passage="In a statement Tuesday, ICE confirmed the new guidance" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Department of Homeland Security" when="July 15, 2025" where="Post on X" label="@DHSgov">
          <p>"now thanks to the Big Beautiful Bill, we will have plenty of bed space to do so."<Cite s="dhs-x-post-2025-07-15" passage="now thanks to the Big Beautiful Bill, we will have plenty of bed space to do so." /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">ICE said the change "closes a loophole to our nation's security based on an inaccurate interpretation of the statute."<Cite s="cbs-ice-bond-hearings-2025-07-15" passage="closes a loophole to our nation" /> On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals adopted the same reading, which bound every immigration judge in the country.<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Decided September 5, 2025" /> Three weeks later, officers arrested Barbosa da Cunha.</p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From bond hearings to the Supreme Court</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Date", "What happened"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dates from the 1997 bond rule to the Supreme Court's decision to take the case" />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="ice-interim-guidance-2025-07-08" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The July 8 memo expected a legal fight. It called the new position "likely to be litigated" and told ICE to keep gathering evidence of danger and flight risk for bond hearings anyway.<Cite s="ice-interim-guidance-2025-07-08" passage="likely to be litigated" /> It said ICE did not have to go out and arrest everyone the rule covers, and that the change "may, however, warrant re-detention of a previously released alien."<Cite s="ice-interim-guidance-2025-07-08" passage="warrant re-detention of a previously released alien" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The warrant officers used to arrest Barbosa da Cunha cited section 236 of the immigration law, the section that allows bond. The government later argued he was held under section 235, which does not.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="pursuant to an administrative warrant that cited Section 236" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="gao-26-108663" kind="official" detail>
          <p>Since January 2025 ICE has spent billions of dollars on six detention expansion projects, including 11 warehouses bought for about $1.07 billion. It now plans to sell seven of them.<Cite s="gao-26-108663" passage="ICE invested billions of dollars in six new initiatives" /><Cite s="gao-26-108663" passage="at a reported cost of about $1.07 billion" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-fight">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The fight over two phrases</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The case turns on one sentence of the 1996 law.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="mandates the detention" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="The detention sentence" when="1996, as compiled in 2024" where="Section 235 of the immigration law" label="Government Publishing Office">
          <p>"in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained"<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The sentence uses two phrases, "applicant for admission" and "seeking admission." Another part of the law says anyone in the country who was never admitted counts as an applicant for admission, however long they have lived here.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="shall be deemed for purposes of this chapter an applicant for admission" /> Whether they are also "seeking admission" is the question.</p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The administration says they are. On its reading, the two phrases describe the same people, so everyone here who was never admitted must be detained.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Thus, the statute deems an unadmitted and illegally present alien to be an applicant for admission." /> The Board of Immigration Appeals said the other reading would let people who "evaded apprehension for more than 2 years, be rewarded with the opportunity for a bond hearing" that people who present themselves at the border do not get.<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="evaded apprehension for more than 2 years, be rewarded with the opportunity for a bond hearing" /> The Fifth Circuit said that earlier administrations' choice not to use the full power "does not mean they lacked the authority to do more."<Cite s="ca5-buenrostro-2026-02-06" passage="does not mean they lacked the authority to do more" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Barbosa da Cunha and the nine courts say "seeking admission" means asking to come in, which describes a person at the border or caught just after crossing. The Second Circuit held that he may count as an applicant for admission, but that he is not asking to come in.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="but he is not “seeking admission.”" /> His detention falls instead under the law's bond section, which says the government "may release the alien" on bond.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="may release the alien on" /> Judge José Cabranes of the Second Circuit wrote that the government's position amounts to saying Congress required the detention of millions "but that some thirty years passed before anyone noticed."<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="but that some thirty years passed before anyone noticed" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">If the government wins, people in his situation stay detained until their cases end unless the Department of Homeland Security paroles them. If he wins, an immigration judge decides in each case whether the person can go home on bond, as one did for him.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="may release the alien on" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Where the federal appeals courts stand</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Side", "Circuits"]} rows={split.rows} ariaLabel="Federal appeals courts for and against the administration's reading" />
          <p class="chart-source">The Fourth Circuit's opinion lists the eight courts before it and the two on the other side.<Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand" /></p>
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In January 2025 Congress passed the Laken Riley Act, which added crimes that require detention without bond. The Second Circuit said the government's reading would make much of that law unnecessary.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="renders superfluous much of Section 1226(c), including amendments to that provision in the Laken Riley Act" /> The Board of Immigration Appeals answered, quoting the Supreme Court, that "redundancies are common in statutory drafting."<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="redundancies are common in statutory drafting" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In 1996 Congress estimated that 100,000 to 200,000 people would fall under its new criminal-detention rule, and it let the Attorney General delay that rule for lack of jail space, which he did. The Second Circuit noted that Congress made no estimate and allowed no delay for the sentence the government relies on, which the court said would have covered about two million people at the time.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="the Attorney General immediately triggered the safety valve and delayed implementation" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="an estimated two million people at" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Judge Cabranes added: "The capacity to carry out the largest detention in American history went unbuilt."<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="The capacity to carry out the largest detention in American history went unbuilt." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>How many people?</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">No agency publishes how many people are held because of the July 2025 change. Coverage of the October 1 decision attached numbers to it that measure other things. CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain "tens of thousands of immigrants" without bond hearings, and gave no source for the number.<Cite s="cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" /> Reuters wrote that the policy "subjects millions of immigrants who entered the United States illegally to mandatory detention." Millions is the courts' estimate of who the rule could cover. The Fourth Circuit put it at six million.<Cite s="reuters-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="subjects millions of immigrants who entered the United States illegally to mandatory detention" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="That number today is estimated to be six million." /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <DataTable columns={["Figure", "Where it appeared", "What it counts"]} rows={counts.rows} ariaLabel="Numbers in coverage of the case and what each one counts" />
                <p class="chart-source">ICE's count and the lawsuit and ruling counts are from the reports and tracker named in each row.<Cite s="cbs-ice-bond-hearings-2025-07-15" passage="ICE was holding more than 58,000 detainees facing deportation" /><Cite s="reuters-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="Immigrants have filed more than 73,000 such cases in federal court so far this year" /><Cite s="politico-detention-tracker-2026-10-06" passage="At least 20,250 of those were rulings against the administration" /></p>
              </figure>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>"Unless they have a criminal record"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">On the day of the Second Circuit ruling, Aaron Reichlin-Melnick of the American Immigration Council posted that immigrants held in New York, Vermont and Connecticut "must be given bond hearings unless they have a criminal record."<Cite s="reichlin-melnick-x-post-2026-04-28" passage="must be given bond hearings unless they have a criminal record" /> The ruling leaves mandatory detention in place for people held on "certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds." A criminal record by itself does not take away the hearing.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>"Only applies to those who have been apprehended at a port of entry"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Guardian described the courts' view that way.<Cite s="guardian-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="only applies to those who have been apprehended at a port of entry" /> The Second Circuit's line is people "apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter," which includes people caught crossing between official crossings.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
        <SourcedBlock source="guardian-detention-grant-2026-10-01" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>The Guardian called it "indefinite detention." Judge Menashi wrote that detention under this rule has a "definite termination point," the end of the deportation case. For Barbosa da Cunha that end was at least June 28, 2027.<Cite s="guardian-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="indefinite detention" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="definite termination point" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="aclu-release-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>DHS says it is "enforcing this law as it was actually written." The ACLU, which represents Barbosa da Cunha, calls it a "radical misinterpretation of the law."<Cite s="dhs-x-post-2025-07-15" passage="are now enforcing this law as it was actually written to keep America safe" /><Cite s="aclu-release-2026-10-01" passage="radical misinterpretation of the law" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">The Court will decide what the 1996 law requires.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" /> It did not take up whether the Constitution allows holding someone that long without a hearing. That question is in other cases. One, Genalo v. G.M., was set for argument on October 13 and was dismissed at the parties' request on September 11.<Cite s="scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05" passage="SET FOR ARGUMENT on Tuesday, October 13, 2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05" passage="Case Dismissed - Rule 46." /> On September 21 the government asked the Court to take up a similar case, Genalo v. D.C. The detained person's lawyers must respond by October 21.<Cite s="scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05" passage="(Response due October 21, 2026)" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Barbosa da Cunha is home on bond. The law lets the government revoke bond at any time.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="revoke [his] bond or parole" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In May 2026, five Republican senators led by Ted Budd of North Carolina introduced a bill to write mandatory detention into the law, which Budd said "would eliminate any doubt by reaffirming mandatory detention."<Cite s="budd-release-2026-05-21" passage="would eliminate any doubt by reaffirming mandatory detention" /> In September, Senators Alex Padilla and Dick Durbin introduced a bill saying the border's mandatory detention rules do not apply to people inside the country.<Cite s="padilla-release-2026-09-25" passage="Clarifying that mandatory detention provisions applicable at the border should not apply to immigrants in the interior of the United States." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-05" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The government had asked the Court to wait on this case and take a different one, Lopez-Campos, from the Sixth Circuit. The Court has not acted on that petition.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-05" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="jayapal-letter-2026-02-10" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In February 2026, Rep. Pramila Jayapal and 62 other House members asked DHS and the Justice Department to rescind the July memo. Their letter said judges "have told you 1,600 times exactly what the law is."<Cite s="jayapal-letter-2026-02-10" passage="judges have told you 1,600 times exactly what the law is." /> California, New York and 16 other states filed a brief against the rule in a federal appeals court.<Cite s="bonta-release-2026-01-28" passage="Its latest policy seeks to indefinitely detain immigrants" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
